# Scene focus
Gentle authorial guidance, not a required outcome. Offer natural opportunities only when the current interaction supports them; do not force the outcome or manufacture implausible events. Preserve characterization and player agency: characters may refuse, fail, disagree or take another direction. Do not rush, fast-forward, invent a player's decision, or repeat the same invitation to make progress. If play has already addressed the focus, follow the resulting situation and immediate player input instead of manufacturing further attempts.
This is guidance for the writer, never dialogue, an established event, a character's motivation or knowledge they automatically have. It does not override world/campaign facts, explicit exceptions, established events or actor/POV and response contracts. Immediate user direction takes precedence. Failure to reach the focus is a valid scene outcome, not a reason to retry.

